      title: "Haiti, Louisiana, and the West",
      body: `## New Orleans

In 1800, by the secret Treaty of San Ildefonso, Spain returned the vast territory of Louisiana, which it had held since 1763, to France, now ruled by **Napoleon Bonaparte**. Napoleon dreamed of rebuilding a French empire in the Americas, with Louisiana as the granary for the sugar island of Saint-Domingue. Jefferson was alarmed. A weak Spain at New Orleans was tolerable; a powerful France was not. In April 1802 he wrote to Robert Livingston, the American minister in Paris, that "there is on the globe one single spot, the possessor of which is our natural and habitual enemy. It is New Orleans." If France took possession, he warned, "we must marry ourselves to the British fleet and nation." In October 1802, the Spanish official at New Orleans, not yet having handed over the colony, suspended the right of Americans to deposit goods there. Westerners clamored for war.

## The Haitian Revolution

What saved the situation was a revolution in the Caribbean. As you saw in Lesson I, enslaved people in Saint-Domingue had risen in 1791, and under the leadership of **Toussaint Louverture**, a formerly enslaved coachman who proved a military and political genius, they had defeated Spanish and British invasions and taken control of the colony, nominally still under France. In 1802 Napoleon sent a great army under his brother-in-law, General Charles Leclerc, to restore French rule and, it soon became clear, slavery. Leclerc captured Toussaint by treachery and shipped him to France, where he died in a prison in the Jura Mountains in April 1803. But the people of Saint-Domingue fought on under **Jean-Jacques Dessalines**, and yellow fever destroyed the French army; Leclerc himself died of it. On January 1, 1804, Dessalines declared the independence of **Haiti**.

![An engraved portrait of Toussaint Louverture in the uniform of a French general.](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Toussaint_L%27Ouverture.jpg "Toussaint Louverture in the uniform of a general of the French Republic, from a series of portraits of revolutionary generals (c. 1800–1802). John Carter Brown Library.")

The defeat in Saint-Domingue ended Napoleon's American ambitions. Without the sugar island, Louisiana was useless to him, and he needed money for a coming war with Britain. In April 1803, his minister Talleyrand startled Livingston and James Monroe, who had been sent to buy New Orleans, by asking what the United States would pay for all of Louisiana.

## The purchase

The American envoys had no authority to buy so much, but they seized the chance. By the treaty of April 30, 1803, the United States bought Louisiana for about $15 million. The territory's boundaries were vague—"I can give you no direction," Talleyrand told them; "you have made a noble bargain for yourselves, and I suppose you will make the most of it"—but it stretched from the Mississippi to the Rocky Mountains, some 828,000 square miles, doubling the size of the United States. The purchase was financed by loans from British and Dutch banks.

The historian Laurent Dubois and others have stressed that Americans owed their greatest territorial acquisition to the formerly enslaved people of Haiti, who destroyed the French army. Yet the United States, under pressure from Southern slaveholders, refused to recognize Haiti, and Jefferson cooperated with a French embargo against it. The United States did not recognize Haiti until 1862.

## Constitutional scruples

The purchase posed a problem for Jefferson. The Constitution gave the federal government no express power to acquire territory or to incorporate its inhabitants as citizens. As a strict constructionist, Jefferson believed that a constitutional amendment was needed, and he drafted one. But his advisers warned that delay might give Napoleon time to change his mind, and Jefferson let the amendment drop, hoping, as he wrote, that "the good sense of our country will correct the evil of construction when it shall produce ill effects." Federalists, who feared that new Western states would swamp New England's influence, opposed the purchase in vain. The Senate ratified the treaty in October 1803.

Louisiana's inhabitants—French, Spanish, African, and Native—were not consulted. The treaty promised them the rights of citizens, but Congress governed the territory of Orleans without an elected legislature at first, and many of its French-speaking residents protested. The territory was admitted as the state of Louisiana in 1812.

## Lewis and Clark

Even before the purchase, Jefferson had asked Congress for money to send an expedition up the Missouri River to the Pacific. He appointed his private secretary, **Meriwether Lewis**, an army officer, to lead it, and Lewis chose as co-commander his friend **William Clark**. Jefferson instructed them to find "the most direct & practicable water communication across this continent, for the purposes of commerce," to record the plants, animals, geography, and peoples they met, and to inform the Native nations that they now had a new "great father" in Washington.

The **Corps of Discovery**, about forty-five men including Clark's enslaved servant **York**, left the St. Louis area in May 1804. They wintered among the Mandans in present-day North Dakota, where they hired a French-Canadian trader, Toussaint Charbonneau, and his young Lemhi Shoshone wife, **Sacagawea**, as interpreters. Sacagawea, carrying her infant son, proved invaluable: her presence signaled to Native peoples that the party was not a war party, and when the expedition reached the Shoshones, whose horses it desperately needed to cross the Rockies, their chief turned out to be her brother. The expedition crossed the Bitterroot Mountains, nearly starving, and descended the Columbia River to the Pacific in November 1805. When they voted on where to spend the winter, Clark recorded the choices of York and of Sacagawea—"Janey," he called her—along with the rest. They returned to St. Louis in September 1806, having lost only one man, to what was probably appendicitis.

The expedition found no easy water route across the continent, for none existed. But it mapped a vast territory, described hundreds of species new to science, established American claims to the Pacific Northwest, and opened the way for traders and, later, settlers. For the Native nations it met, it was the beginning of the end of their independence. York asked for his freedom after the expedition; Clark refused for years and finally freed him around 1816.

> **Pause and consider.** Jefferson the strict constructionist bought Louisiana without constitutional authority. Was he right to do so? What does the episode suggest about the relationship between principle and power?`,
